\begin{remark}
Of the twenty fibers tested, none has rank $0$, eleven have rank $\ge 2$, and
one has rank $3$. The average is far above the conjectural average rank
($1/2$) of generic elliptic curves; part of this is selection bias (fibers
displayed here are precisely those carrying integral points), but the
systematic appearance of rank $2$ among the large-$|n|$ fibers
($16531$, $17309$, $27949$, $-4741$, $-6561$) found by the divisor-pair sweep
suggests the family's rank distribution merits direct study, e.g.\ a root
number / rank census over all $|n|\le 10^{3}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Each "second generator not yet found" is a concrete target: it exists
(the rank is unconditional), it has canonical height beyond naive search
to $H=10^{5}$, and finding it would enable group-law enumeration that may
produce new large integral points on that fiber --- the mechanism that
presumably created the known giant $(n,m)=(798,\,-5378466956142)$.
Candidate methods: higher \texttt{ellrank} effort, $4$-descent, or
Heegner-point techniques after twisting.
\end{remark}

